---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Drainage"
	description="Simple, low-cost drainage solutions to keep Irish golf courses playable through wet winters - engineering advice from Maurice McKinley BSc CEng MICE."
>
	<section class="section section--tight hero">
		<div class="container">
			<span class="eyebrow">Drainage</span>
			<h1>Simple, low-cost ways to get your drainage working effectively</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				Every winter that a course stays waterlogged is lost revenue, lost play, and a course
				that recovers more slowly in spring. Drainage is usually the single most effective
				investment a club can make. The aim is always to get the system working again with the
				minimum intervention needed, so it pays for itself, rather than starting with a complete
				overhaul, with all the cost and disruption that would cause.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section--alt">
		<div class="container">
			<span class="eyebrow">Why it matters more now</span>
			<h2>Wet winters, short seasons</h2>
			<p class="lede">
				Irish courses often lose many days of their playing season to saturated fairways and
				soft ground, especially in winter. Effective drainage is the most direct way to
				improve course condition and playability, and the benefits go further: less machinery
				time spent restoring surfaces, less cost in materials and labour for repairs, more
				green fee income, and more days of play in an increasingly wet climate.
			</p>
			<a href="/sustainability/">See the wider Sustainability programme →</a>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="grid grid--3">
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Diagnosis first</h3>
					<p>
						Some drainage problems have an obvious, identifiable cause: a blocked outfall, a
						failed joint, but more often simply a sealed gravel drain that hasn't been
						maintained or even identified. Fixing the right thing can often cost a fraction of
						a comprehensive new drainage system, which is rarely needed.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Targeted Improvements</h3>
					<p>
						From local drainage work on problem greens and fairways to improving and extending
						the existing system, the aim is maximum long-term improvement for the lowest
						capital spend, reducing future maintenance costs along the way.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Slopes and Structures</h3>
					<p>
						Whether dealing with slope stability or built structures such as bridges, culverts,
						and retaining features, engineering advice can include preparing tender documents
						and procuring consultants and contractors where needed.
					</p>
					<a href="/services/structures/">See Structures →</a>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container">
			<span class="eyebrow">In practice</span>
			<h2>Real photos from Recent Projects</h2>
			<div class="grid grid--3">
				<figure style="margin:0;">
					<img
						src="/images/gallery/drains-by-excavator.png"
						alt="A low-pressure tracked excavator laying a deep drain on a fairway"
						style="width:100%; height:14rem; object-fit:cover; border-radius:8px;"
					/>
					<figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem; font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--color-body);">
						A low-pressure tracked excavator, often the best way to lay deep or large drains.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure style="margin:0;">
					<img
						src="/images/gallery/trencher.jpg"
						alt="A narrow bucket trencher installing a drain"
						style="width:100%; height:14rem; object-fit:cover; border-radius:8px;"
					/>
					<figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem; font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--color-body);">
						A narrow bucket trencher installs drains quickly and neatly where the ground allows.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure style="margin:0;">
					<img
						src="/images/gallery/refreshed-drain.jpg"
						alt="An old drain refreshed by skimming off the top turf and replacing with gravel"
						style="width:100%; height:14rem; object-fit:cover; border-radius:8px;"
					/>
					<figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem; font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--color-body);">
						Old drains refreshed simply and cheaply by replacing the surface gravel.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container" style="display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:1.5rem; align-items:center; justify-content:space-between;">
			<div>
				<h2 style="margin-top:0;">Free introductory consultation</h2>
				<p style="margin-bottom:0; max-width:34rem;">
					A half-day, no-obligation site visit to identify your course's drainage priorities and
					the lowest-cost path to fixing them.
				</p>
			</div>
			<a class="btn btn--primary" href="/contact/">Book a consultation</a>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
